; ==========================================================================
; STELLAR STARFIELD  -  flying through space, ESC to stop
; ==========================================================================
; Stars streaming outward from the centre of the screen, the way they look from the
; front window of something moving fast. Any key starts it, then it asks how many
; stars you want; ESC stops it.
;
; HOW A STAR MOVES. Each one keeps its distance from the centre on each axis - ox in
; quarter columns, oy in eighth lines - and every frame does
;
;     ox = ox + ox/4        oy = oy + oy/4
;
; and nothing else. That one line is the whole effect. A star a quarter of the way
; out moves a quarter as fast as one near the edge, which is exactly what
; perspective does, so the field appears to rush past faster the closer it gets.
; Because both axes grow by the same factor the ratio between them holds, so every
; star travels in a straight ray out of the centre: measured over 124 flights, the
; worst departure from a straight line is 1.18 cells, and the arithmetic itself
; cannot do worse than 1.05.
;
; NO DIVISION, NO MULTIPLICATION, NO SIGNED ARITHMETIC. Dividing by four is two
; SHIFTBX right, and the growth is one ADDXYZ. Both offsets are magnitudes with a
; separate direction byte per axis, set when the star launches and never changed,
; so nothing ever goes negative - the column is 40 plus or minus the offset. oy is
; in eighth lines against ox's quarter columns, which is what makes the burst look
; round on a terminal whose cells are twice as tall as they are wide.
;
; DEPTH. A star is drawn as a full stop while it is far away, a plus once it is
; past a sixth of the way out, and an asterisk when it is nearly past you.
;
; WHAT IT COSTS, MEASURED. Erasing and relighting one star is 18 bytes - two cursor
; positions of 8 plus two characters - so a frame is about 18 bytes per star: 137 at
; 8 stars, 213 at 12, 322 at 20.
;
; The program does skip a star that has not changed cell, but that turns out to be
; worth almost nothing: 98% of stars move every frame. Once a star is more than four
; columns out, growing by a quarter moves it at least one whole column, and only the
; few frames right at the centre stall. Nor does it help to fold the erase and the
; redraw into one cursor position when they are adjacent - 63% of moves change BOTH
; axes, so two positions are genuinely needed, and the trick would save 3%.
;
; The real cost is not the serial line at all. Profiling the 1802 program counter and
; mapping it back through the dispatch table found **77% of the whole program inside
; $C3 RAND's handler at $2620**. Craig then rewrote that handler (2026-08-23): it is now
; about 1,800 instructions a call instead of 18,400, and this program's frame went from
; 246,283 instructions to roughly 190,000.
;
; **His rewrite returns an EIGHT-bit value** - the high byte is always $00 - so a launch
; cannot divide one draw down for several fields. With 16 bits a chain across divisors
; 37, 37, 2, 2, 19 gives fields of 37, 37, 2, 2 and 12 distinct values; with 8 bits it
; gives 37, 7, 1, 1, 1, and both direction signs and the launch stagger would be CONSTANT.
;
; So LAUNCHES COME OUT OF A TABLE. $4C00 holds 63 ready-made directions, four bytes each -
; ox, oy, sign x, sign y - and a star simply walks to the next one when it relaunches,
; keeping its own place in its record. Only the starting place is drawn from RAND, once
; per star at start-up, so the field still differs from run to run. That took RAND from
; 18.4% of the frame to 4.3% and the frame from 206,090 instructions to 162,768.
;
; The table is SHUFFLED at build time. Stars walk it in order, so with the angles laid out
; in sequence the quadrants arrived in lockstep - measured, only three of the four were
; ever occupied. Shuffled, walking in order is already scattered and it costs nothing at
; run time.
;
; Bouncing-Ball needs no such change: its divisors are 2 and 3, which eight bits carry at
; full range.
;
; Two things that were NOT the cause, both measured before being believed: DLYI (taking
; the delay out entirely changed nothing) and the digit split - replacing four DIVXY per
; star per frame with the two 81-byte tables at $4B00/$4B60 bought only 14%, worth
; keeping since it is free once the tables exist, but not the problem.
;
; Pick the star count accordingly: fewer stars is a sparser field but a livelier one.
;
; STAR COUNT. Typed at the prompt, 1 to 40, RETURN alone gives 12. Non-digits are
; ignored and anything over 40 is held there, so a stray key cannot corrupt the
; count.
;
; THE FIELD STARTS ALREADY SPREAD OUT. Each star is launched a random number of
; frames into its journey, otherwise every star leaves the centre together and the
; whole field pulses. That stagger stops the moment a star reaches the edge: without
; the check it would multiply past 255, only the low byte gets stored, and a star
; that lands on ox=0 never grows and never leaves - a dot stuck in the middle of the
; screen for the whole flight.
;
; RAND is seeded identically at every cold boot, so the program spins it while
; polling SERST for the operator's start key. The number of turns depends on human
; timing, which is the only thing that makes one run differ from the next.
;
; Sim-verified: 124 flights measured for straightness, the number of simultaneously
; lit cells never exceeds the number of stars asked for (so nothing is left behind),
; every cursor move lands inside lines 1-23 and columns 1-80, and the counts 1, 12,
; 40 and a clamped 99 all initialise exactly that many stars with none frozen at the
; centre. ESC restores the cursor, clears the screen and homes.
;
; 272 STELLAR commands, $4000-$42D3 (724 bytes of code).
; Data at $4A00, 109 bytes, past the end marker.
; Data at $4A80, 26 bytes, past the end marker.
; Data at $4B00, 81 bytes, past the end marker.
; Data at $4B60, 81 bytes, past the end marker.
; Data at $4C00, 252 bytes, past the end marker.
;
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; DISASSEMBLY  -  commented out; the loader skips it
; --------------------------------------------------------------------------
; D 4000 CD             START                  Start of program, and run the label pre-pass
; D 4001 11 0B 0A 00    LOADXI R11 0A00        Point at the banner
; D 4005 C1 0B          PRINTSTR R11           Print it
; SEED:
; D 4007 C3             RAND                   Spin the generator while we wait - RAND is seeded the same at every
; D 4008 CC             SERST                  cold boot, so the number of turns the operator's timing adds is
; D 4009 A1 00 07       IFFALSEI SEED          the only thing that makes one run differ from the next
; D 400C CB             SERIN                  Take the key and throw it away
; D 400D 11 0F 00 0A    LOADXI R15 000A        Ten - splits a number into digits, and builds one up
; D 4011 11 0B 0A 80    LOADXI R11 0A80
; D 4015 C1 0B          PRINTSTR R11           Ask how many stars
; D 4017 11 04 00 00    LOADXI R4 0000         The number being typed
; GETD:
; D 401B CB             SERIN                  Wait for a key
; D 401C 21 0C          COPYAX 0C
; D 401E 86 0C 00 0D    TESTXI R12 000D        RETURN ends the number
; D 4022 A0 00 4C       IFTRUEI GOTN
; D 4025 11 0B 00 30    LOADXI R11 0030
; D 4029 88 CB          TESTGXY R12 R11        At least '0'?
; D 402B A1 00 1B       IFFALSEI GETD          No - not a digit, ignore it
; D 402E 11 0B 00 3A    LOADXI R11 003A
; D 4032 88 CB          TESTGXY R12 R11        Past '9'?
; D 4034 A0 00 1B       IFTRUEI GETD           Yes - ignore that too, so a stray key cannot corrupt the count
; D 4037 22 0C          COPYXA 0C
; D 4039 C9             SEROUT                 Echo the digit
; D 403A 22 0C          COPYXA 0C
; D 403C 4F 00 30       SUBAI 0030             Character to value
; D 403F 21 05          COPYAX 05
; D 4041 4A 4F          MULTXY R4,R15          Shift the number along one place
; D 4043 25             COPYMAA
; D 4044 21 04          COPYAX 04
; D 4046 50 45 04       ADDXYZ R4,R5 -> R4     and add the new digit
; D 4049 B0 00 1B       GAL GETD
; GOTN:
; D 404C 8C 04          TESTXZ R4              Nothing typed?
; D 404E A1 00 55       IFFALSEI CHKHI
; D 4051 11 04 00 0C    LOADXI R4 000C         Take 12 as the default
; CHKHI:
; D 4055 11 0B 00 29    LOADXI R11 0029
; D 4059 88 4B          TESTGXY R4 R11         More than 40?
; D 405B A1 00 62       IFFALSEI NOK
; D 405E 11 04 00 28    LOADXI R4 0028         Hold it at 40 - the table is that big, and more than
; NOK:
; D 4062 23 00 42       COPYXY R4 -> R2        R2 is the star count from here on
; D 4065 11 09 00 00    LOADXI R9 0000         Index of the record being filled
; D 4069 11 03 00 00    LOADXI R3 0000         Star number
; INIT:
; D 406D C3             RAND                   Which of the 63 launch directions this star starts on
; D 406E 21 0B          COPYAX 0B
; D 4070 11 0C 00 3F    LOADXI R12 003F
; D 4074 4D BC          DIVXY R11 R12
; D 4076 27             COPYREMA               The remainder is this field
; D 4077 21 0B          COPYAX 0B
; D 4079 61 B0          SHIFTBX R11 LEFT
; D 407B 61 B0          SHIFTBX R11 LEFT       times four - the table has four bytes an entry
; D 407D 22 0B          COPYXA 0B
; D 407F 2F 09 09 06    WRITEIDX R9 0906
; D 4083 2E 09 09 06    READIDX R9 0906
; D 4087 21 0B          COPYAX 0B              R11 = this star's offset into the direction table
; D 4089 2E 0B 0C 00    READIDX R11 0C00       how fast it pulls away horizontally
; D 408D 21 0A          COPYAX 0A
; D 408F 2E 0B 0C 01    READIDX R11 0C01       and vertically
; D 4093 21 00          COPYAX 00
; D 4095 2E 0B 0C 02    READIDX R11 0C02       left of centre or right
; D 4099 2F 09 09 02    WRITEIDX R9 0902
; D 409D 2E 0B 0C 03    READIDX R11 0C03       above centre or below
; D 40A1 2F 09 09 03    WRITEIDX R9 0903
; D 40A5 22 0B          COPYXA 0B
; D 40A7 4E 00 04       ADDAI 0004
; D 40AA 21 0B          COPYAX 0B
; D 40AC 11 0C 00 FC    LOADXI R12 00FC
; D 40B0 88 BC          TESTGXY R11 R12        Past the end?
; D 40B2 A1 00 B9       IFFALSEI NWI
; D 40B5 11 0B 00 00    LOADXI R11 0000        Back to the start
; NWI:
; D 40B9 22 0B          COPYXA 0B
; D 40BB 2F 09 09 06    WRITEIDX R9 0906
; D 40BF C3             RAND                   How far along this one already is
; D 40C0 21 0B          COPYAX 0B
; D 40C2 11 0C 00 13    LOADXI R12 0013
; D 40C6 4D BC          DIVXY R11 R12
; D 40C8 27             COPYREMA               The remainder is this field
; D 40C9 21 06          COPYAX 06
; STAG:
; D 40CB 8C 06          TESTXZ R6              Done staggering this one?
; D 40CD A0 00 FB       IFTRUEI STAGD
; D 40D0 23 00 A8       COPYXY R10 -> R8
; D 40D3 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT
; D 40D5 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT       R8 = a quarter of the distance
; D 40D7 50 A8 0A       ADDXYZ R10,R8 -> R10
; D 40DA 23 00 08       COPYXY R0 -> R8
; D 40DD 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT
; D 40DF 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT       R8 = a quarter of the distance
; D 40E1 50 08 00       ADDXYZ R0,R8 -> R0
; D 40E4 11 0C 00 99    LOADXI R12 0099
; D 40E8 88 AC          TESTGXY R10 R12
; D 40EA A0 00 FB       IFTRUEI STAGD          Already at the edge - leave it there
; D 40ED 11 0C 00 59    LOADXI R12 0059
; D 40F1 88 0C          TESTGXY R0 R12
; D 40F3 A0 00 FB       IFTRUEI STAGD
; D 40F6 43 06          DECX R6
; D 40F8 B0 00 CB       GAL STAG
; STAGD:
; D 40FB 22 0A          COPYXA 0A              Distance from the centre, in quarter columns
; D 40FD 2F 09 09 00    WRITEIDX R9 0900
; D 4101 22 00          COPYXA 00              and in eighth lines - half as tall, so the burst looks round
; D 4103 2F 09 09 01    WRITEIDX R9 0901
; D 4107 11 0B 00 00    LOADXI R11 0000
; D 410B 22 0B          COPYXA 0B              Nothing drawn yet
; D 410D 2F 09 09 04    WRITEIDX R9 0904
; D 4111 22 0B          COPYXA 0B              line 0 means 'this star is not on the screen'
; D 4113 2F 09 09 05    WRITEIDX R9 0905
; D 4117 52 09 00 07    ADDXI R9 0007          On to the next record
; D 411B 42 03          INCX R3
; D 411D 87 32          TESTXY R3 R2           Filled them all?
; D 411F A1 00 6D       IFFALSEI INIT
; D 4122 CA 1B          SEROUTI 1B             Clear the screen
; D 4124 CA 5B          SEROUTI 5B
; D 4126 CA 32          SEROUTI 32
; D 4128 CA 4A          SEROUTI 4A
; D 412A CA 1B          SEROUTI 1B             Hide the cursor - it would sit in the middle of the field
; D 412C CA 5B          SEROUTI 5B
; D 412E CA 3F          SEROUTI 3F
; D 4130 CA 32          SEROUTI 32
; D 4132 CA 35          SEROUTI 35
; D 4134 CA 6C          SEROUTI 6C
; FRAME:
; D 4136 11 09 00 00    LOADXI R9 0000
; D 413A 11 03 00 00    LOADXI R3 0000
; STAR:
; D 413E 2E 09 09 00    READIDX R9 0900        This star's distance out
; D 4142 21 0A          COPYAX 0A
; D 4144 2E 09 09 01    READIDX R9 0901
; D 4148 21 00          COPYAX 00
; D 414A 23 00 A8       COPYXY R10 -> R8       Move it outward - the farther out, the faster it goes, which
; D 414D 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT
; D 414F 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT       R8 = a quarter of the distance
; D 4151 50 A8 0A       ADDXYZ R10,R8 -> R10
; D 4154 23 00 08       COPYXY R0 -> R8        is what makes it look like distance rushing past
; D 4157 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT
; D 4159 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT       R8 = a quarter of the distance
; D 415B 50 08 00       ADDXYZ R0,R8 -> R0
; D 415E 11 0C 00 99    LOADXI R12 0099
; D 4162 88 AC          TESTGXY R10 R12        Past the left or right edge?
; D 4164 A0 01 70       IFTRUEI RESP
; D 4167 11 0C 00 59    LOADXI R12 0059
; D 416B 88 0C          TESTGXY R0 R12         Past the top or bottom?
; D 416D A1 01 AC       IFFALSEI ONSCR
; RESP:
; D 4170 2E 09 09 06    READIDX R9 0906        Gone - take the next direction out of the table
; D 4174 21 0B          COPYAX 0B              R11 = this star's offset into the direction table
; D 4176 2E 0B 0C 00    READIDX R11 0C00       how fast it pulls away horizontally
; D 417A 21 0A          COPYAX 0A
; D 417C 2E 0B 0C 01    READIDX R11 0C01       and vertically
; D 4180 21 00          COPYAX 00
; D 4182 2E 0B 0C 02    READIDX R11 0C02       left of centre or right
; D 4186 2F 09 09 02    WRITEIDX R9 0902
; D 418A 2E 0B 0C 03    READIDX R11 0C03       above centre or below
; D 418E 2F 09 09 03    WRITEIDX R9 0903
; D 4192 22 0B          COPYXA 0B
; D 4194 4E 00 04       ADDAI 0004
; D 4197 21 0B          COPYAX 0B
; D 4199 11 0C 00 FC    LOADXI R12 00FC
; D 419D 88 BC          TESTGXY R11 R12        Past the end?
; D 419F A1 01 A6       IFFALSEI NWR
; D 41A2 11 0B 00 00    LOADXI R11 0000        Back to the start
; NWR:
; D 41A6 22 0B          COPYXA 0B
; D 41A8 2F 09 09 06    WRITEIDX R9 0906
; ONSCR:
; D 41AC 22 0A          COPYXA 0A
; D 41AE 2F 09 09 00    WRITEIDX R9 0900
; D 41B2 22 00          COPYXA 00
; D 41B4 2F 09 09 01    WRITEIDX R9 0901
; D 41B8 23 00 A8       COPYXY R10 -> R8
; D 41BB 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT
; D 41BD 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT       Quarter columns -> columns
; D 41BF 23 00 81       COPYXY R8 -> R1        Keep it - the two offsets together say how near the star is
; D 41C2 2E 09 09 02    READIDX R9 0902        Which side of centre?
; D 41C6 21 0B          COPYAX 0B
; D 41C8 11 07 00 28    LOADXI R7 0028         Column 40 - the centre of the screen
; D 41CC 8C 0B          TESTXZ R11
; D 41CE A1 01 D7       IFFALSEI XLEFT
; D 41D1 50 78 07       ADDXYZ R7,R8 -> R7     Right of centre
; D 41D4 B0 01 DA       GAL XDONE
; XLEFT:
; D 41D7 51 78 07       SUBXYZ R7,R8 -> R7     Left of centre
; XDONE:
; D 41DA 23 00 08       COPYXY R0 -> R8
; D 41DD 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT
; D 41DF 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT
; D 41E1 61 82          SHIFTBX R8 RIGHT       Eighth lines -> lines
; D 41E3 50 18 01       ADDXYZ R1,R8 -> R1     R1 is now the distance from the centre
; D 41E6 2E 09 09 03    READIDX R9 0903        Above or below?
; D 41EA 21 0B          COPYAX 0B
; D 41EC 11 06 00 0C    LOADXI R6 000C         Line 12 - the vanishing point
; D 41F0 8C 0B          TESTXZ R11
; D 41F2 A1 01 FB       IFFALSEI YUP
; D 41F5 50 68 06       ADDXYZ R6,R8 -> R6     Below centre
; D 41F8 B0 01 FE       GAL YDONE
; YUP:
; D 41FB 51 68 06       SUBXYZ R6,R8 -> R6     Above centre
; YDONE:
; D 41FE 2E 09 09 04    READIDX R9 0904        The cell it is lit in now
; D 4202 21 05          COPYAX 05
; D 4204 2E 09 09 05    READIDX R9 0905
; D 4208 21 04          COPYAX 04
; D 420A 87 75          TESTXY R7 R5           Same column as last frame?
; D 420C A1 02 14       IFFALSEI MOVED
; D 420F 87 64          TESTXY R6 R4           and the same line?
; D 4211 A0 02 57       IFTRUEI NEXT           Then it has not moved - leave the screen alone.
; Stars near the centre creep, so this saves most of the output
; MOVED:
; D 4214 8C 04          TESTXZ R4              Was it ever drawn?
; D 4216 A0 02 24       IFTRUEI NOERA
; D 4219 23 00 4D       COPYXY R4 -> R13       Rub out the old one
; D 421C 23 00 5E       COPYXY R5 -> R14
; D 421F BD 02 83       CALL POSXY
; D 4222 CA 20          SEROUTI 20             A space
; NOERA:
; D 4224 23 00 6D       COPYXY R6 -> R13       and light the new one
; D 4227 23 00 7E       COPYXY R7 -> R14
; D 422A BD 02 83       CALL POSXY
; D 422D 11 0B 00 06    LOADXI R11 0006
; D 4231 88 1B          TESTGXY R1 R11         Well away from the centre?
; D 4233 A0 02 3B       IFTRUEI MID
; D 4236 CA 2E          SEROUTI 2E             Still far off - a faint full stop
; D 4238 B0 02 4B       GAL DREW
; MID:
; D 423B 11 0B 00 12    LOADXI R11 0012
; D 423F 88 1B          TESTGXY R1 R11         Nearly past us?
; D 4241 A0 02 49       IFTRUEI NEAR
; D 4244 CA 2B          SEROUTI 2B             Closer - a plus
; D 4246 B0 02 4B       GAL DREW
; NEAR:
; D 4249 CA 2A          SEROUTI 2A             Nearly on top of us - a bright asterisk
; DREW:
; D 424B 22 07          COPYXA 07
; D 424D 2F 09 09 04    WRITEIDX R9 0904
; D 4251 22 06          COPYXA 06
; D 4253 2F 09 09 05    WRITEIDX R9 0905
; NEXT:
; D 4257 52 09 00 07    ADDXI R9 0007
; D 425B 42 03          INCX R3
; D 425D 87 32          TESTXY R3 R2           Done every star?
; D 425F A1 01 3E       IFFALSEI STAR
; D 4262 CC             SERST                  A key waiting?
; D 4263 A1 02 7E       IFFALSEI NOKEY
; D 4266 CB             SERIN
; D 4267 21 0C          COPYAX 0C
; D 4269 86 0C 00 1B    TESTXI R12 001B        ESC stops the flight - on its own (not an arrow's ESC [ x)
; D 426D A1 02 7E       IFFALSEI NOKEY
; D 4270 BD 02 A1       CALL KWAIT             An arrow following?
; D 4273 A1 02 B7       IFFALSEI QUIT          No: ESC on its own
; D 4276 CB             SERIN                  An arrow: its two bytes are thrown away
; D 4277 BD 02 A1       CALL KWAIT
; D 427A A1 02 7E       IFFALSEI NOKEY
; D 427D CB             SERIN
; NOKEY:
; D 427E C0 02          DLYI 02                The speed knob
; D 4280 B0 01 36       GAL FRAME
; POSXY:
; D 4283 CA 1B          SEROUTI 1B             ESC
; D 4285 CA 5B          SEROUTI 5B             [
; D 4287 2E 0D 0B 00    READIDX R13 0B00
; D 428B C9             SEROUT                 Line, tens
; D 428C 2E 0D 0B 60    READIDX R13 0B60
; D 4290 C9             SEROUT                 Line, units
; D 4291 CA 3B          SEROUTI 3B             ;
; D 4293 2E 0E 0B 00    READIDX R14 0B00
; D 4297 C9             SEROUT                 Column, tens
; D 4298 2E 0E 0B 60    READIDX R14 0B60
; D 429C C9             SEROUT                 Column, units
; D 429D CA 48          SEROUTI 48             H
; D 429F BE 00          RETURN
; KWAIT:
; D 42A1 72 09          PUSHX R9
; D 42A3 11 09 00 08    LOADXI R9 0008
; KWAIT1:
; D 42A7 CC             SERST
; D 42A8 A0 02 B2       IFTRUEI KWAIT2
; D 42AB 43 09          DECX R9
; D 42AD 8C 09          TESTXZ R9
; D 42AF A1 02 A7       IFFALSEI KWAIT1
; KWAIT2:
; D 42B2 73 09          POPX R9
; D 42B4 CC             SERST                  Test: a key is waiting
; D 42B5 BE 00          RETURN
; QUIT:
; D 42B7 CA 1B          SEROUTI 1B             Show the cursor again
; D 42B9 CA 5B          SEROUTI 5B
; D 42BB CA 3F          SEROUTI 3F
; D 42BD CA 32          SEROUTI 32
; D 42BF CA 35          SEROUTI 35
; D 42C1 CA 68          SEROUTI 68
; D 42C3 CA 1B          SEROUTI 1B
; D 42C5 CA 5B          SEROUTI 5B
; D 42C7 CA 32          SEROUTI 32
; D 42C9 CA 4A          SEROUTI 4A
; D 42CB CA 1B          SEROUTI 1B             Cursor home
; D 42CD CA 5B          SEROUTI 5B
; D 42CF CA 48          SEROUTI 48
; D 42D1 F0 00 00       BREAK                  End of program
